// Fused Q4_K_M matvec -- DEPRECATED. Use lib/std/matmul_q4k.quanta.
//
// This file was WRONG in four independent ways and never worked. Kept only
// as a record of the failures, because each one produces plausible garbage
// rather than a crash, which is the expensive kind of bug to rediscover.
//
// 1. BLOCK SIZE. The comments talked themselves into 146 bytes:
//      "2 bytes scale (f16) + 128 bytes quads = 144 bytes per 256 elements
//       Actually ... = 146 bytes?
//       Let me use the standard: Q4_K = 2 + 16 + 128 = 146 bytes
//       But the file uses Q4_K_M which is 2 + 2 + 128 = 132 bytes?"
//    The real super-block is 144 bytes:
//      +0  f16 d        (2)   scale for sub-block scales
//      +2  f16 dmin     (2)   scale for sub-block mins
//      +4  u8 scales[12] (12) eight (scale,min) 6-bit pairs, bit-packed
//      +16 u8 qs[128]   (128) 4-bit quants, low nibble even / high odd
//    A 146 stride walks 2 bytes out of alignment per block, so every block
//    after the first reads garbage.
//
// 2. FIELD OFFSETS. It read quads at +18 with a 16-byte scale table. Both are
//    off: the table is 12 bytes at +4, quads are at +16.
//
// 3. NIBBLE SEMANTICS. It used (nibble - 8), which is Q4_0's signed
//    4-bit-with-bias-8 encoding. Q4_K nibbles are UNSIGNED 0..15; the
//    negative direction comes entirely from the per-sub-block min term.
//    Applying a -8 bias here is roughly a factor-of-2 scale error on every
//    weight.
//
// 4. NO SCALE BIT-PACKING. The 12-byte table holds 6-bit (scale, min) pairs
//    whose layout differs above and below index 4:
//      j < 4 : sc[j] & 63            -> scale
//              sc[j+4] & 63           -> min
//      j >= 4: sc[j+4] & 0xF          scale low nibble
//              (sc[j-4] >> 6) << 4    scale high bits   <- j-4, not j
//              sc[j+4] >> 4           min low nibble
//              (sc[j]   >> 6) << 4    min high bits      <- j,   not j-4
//    This file used the raw byte as a scale, ignoring the min entirely, so
//    even with the right offsets the weights would be wrong.
//
// The verified replacement is lib/std/matmul_q4k.quanta. Its
// q4k_matvec_real() dequantizes each 144-byte super-block on the fly (never
// materializing an f32 weight copy) and is bit-for-bit identical to Python
// on the same bytes -- see test_suites/codes/q4k_matvec_test.quanta, which
// also asserts that the compiler's own q4k_matvec builtin is still a stub.
//
// matvec_q4k() is kept as a thin alias so any existing caller keeps working,
// but it is no longer an independent implementation.

import std/gguf
import std/matmul_q4k

fn matvec_q4k(W_raw, x, rows, cols, y) -> i64 {
    return q4k_matvec_real(W_raw, x, rows, cols, y)
}